### Tone: Rule slide: closeness is measured in "times off". Twice too high is as good as twice too low. Patient,
### patronising explanation, as to a slow intern.

rules-times-off-1 = Closeness is measured in times off. Twice too high is exactly as bad as twice too low. Symmetry. You’re welcome.
rules-times-off-2 = I don’t care how many units you missed by. I care how many *times*. Half the answer is as wrong as double it.
rules-times-off-3 = Being off by a factor of ten is one order of magnitude. Being off by a factor of a million is a lifestyle. Aim lower. Or higher. Aim *closer*.
rules-times-off-4 = Being twice too big and being half as big are the same mistake, mathematically. Emotionally, too.
rules-times-off-5 = I measure error in multiples, not units. Off by a few thousand on a billion is superb. Off by double on a dozen is not.
rules-times-off-6 = Don’t chase the exact number. Chase the right order of magnitude. Exactness is a privilege you haven’t earned.
rules-times-off-7 = Overshoot or undershoot, I don’t mind which. I only care by how many times. It’s the fairest way to disappoint you.
rules-times-off-8 = The number line ahead is logarithmic: every step is ten times the last. Being ten times off is one whole step of shame.
rules-times-off-9 = Too high, too low: irrelevant. Times off: everything. Please memorise this. There will be a test. This is the test.
rules-times-off-10 = Double the truth or half of it: same distance, same score. It’s perfectly fair. I find fairness very efficient.
rules-times-off-11 = Ten times too big, ten times too small: identical crimes. I sentence them equally.
rules-times-off-12 = Forget the units. Count the multiples. Off by double is double trouble, whichever way it goes.
rules-times-off-13 = Missing a billion by a million is excellent. Missing seven by seven hundred is not. Ratios, not differences.
rules-times-off-14 = I measure how many times wrong you are, not how far. It’s kinder to big numbers. And crueller to small ones.
rules-times-off-15 = Half is as bad as double. A tenth is as bad as ten times. The number line doesn’t care which side you fall off.
